UPDATE tools SET description = $x$Kling AI is a text- and image-to-video generator from Kuaishou. Kling 4.0 officially launched on October 15, 2026, with Kling 4.0 Flash in early access since September 28, 2026 for Ultra yearly subscribers; the legacy Kling 1.0 through 2.1 Master models, the Virtual Try-On API and the 119 effect templates were retired in September 2026.$x$
 WHERE slug = 'kling-ai' AND lang = 'en';

UPDATE tools SET description_long = $x$Kling AI is a video generation platform built by the Chinese company Kuaishou. You describe a scene in text, or feed it a still image, and it animates a short cinematic clip. Over the past two years it has grown from an experimental demo into one of the most widely used AI video tools.

The current generation is Kling 4.0, which succeeded Kling 3.0 and officially launched on October 15, 2026, according to Kuaishou's own press release. Kling 4.0 Flash entered early access on September 28, 2026, available to Ultra yearly subscribers, generating clips of up to 20 seconds at 720p with 8-bit SDR. Kuaishou also retired the legacy Kling 1.0 through 2.1 Master models, the Virtual Try-On API and the 119 effect templates in September 2026.

Kuaishou's official materials for Kling 4.0 list single-pass clips of up to 30 seconds, up to 10 keyframes per generation, an Omni Reference feature supporting up to 15 reference items (up to 10 images, 5 videos and 7 subjects) for consistent characters and objects, two-channel stereo audio, support for a 21:9 aspect ratio, and prompts of up to roughly 8,000 tokens. The press release also describes 4K, 1080p and 21:9 output and up to 10-bit HDR, though an earlier Kling model-release note still lists 4K and HDR as 'coming soon' rather than confirmed live across all plans. A video-extension feature aimed at roughly two-minute videos, and a developer API for enterprises, are both described as coming soon rather than currently available.

Kling AI uses a tiered, credit-based subscription model, with separate plans such as Standard, Pro, Premier and Ultra that unlock larger monthly credit allowances, and annual billing available at a discount on some tiers. Specific current pricing for Kling 4.0 and Flash has not been published in Kuaishou's launch materials, and the previously published rates for the 3.0-era plans could not be reconfirmed at the time of this review.

The main limitations right now are the lack of published pricing for the new model, inconsistency across Kuaishou's own materials about whether 4K and HDR output are actually live for all users or still rolling out, and the fact that the roughly two-minute video extension and a full developer API are announced but not yet available. Kling 4.0 Flash early access is also gated behind an Ultra-tier yearly subscription, and no free plan was confirmed.

Kling suits creators, marketers and video editors who want high-quality short clips for social content, ads or B-roll, and who are comfortable working shot by shot while some advanced output options such as full HDR and 4K continue to roll out.$x$
 WHERE slug = 'kling-ai' AND lang = 'en';

